  "image_name": "0c3097888b730c05d183e945651c07eb.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes how breaking up prolonged sitting with bouts of walking appears to improve several cardiovascular and metabolic outcomes. Across the top panels, the key theme is that walking increases blood flow and shear stress along the vessel wall. In the vascular function section, higher shear stress is linked to more nitric oxide and less endothelin-1, shifting the vessel toward smooth muscle relaxation rather than constriction. A similar comparison is shown for blood pressure: uninterrupted sitting is associated with a pattern of higher endothelin-1 and lower nitric oxide, whereas interrupting sitting with walking shows the opposite balance, consistent with lower vascular tone and better pressure control.\n\nThe lower left panels extend this idea to glucose handling. During prolonged sitting, blood glucose remains high and relatively little glucose enters muscle, while walking breaks seem to promote GLUT4-mediated uptake into muscle cells, leaving blood glucose at a more normal level. The lower right panel connects these effects to the brain, suggesting that hyperglycaemia, hypertension, and vascular dysfunction can narrow arteries and impair cerebral blood flow, whereas movement breaks are associated with a wider vessel and better brain perfusion. Overall, the figure presents walking interruptions as protective compared with continuous sitting.",
